WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.367
-Welcome back to the show
It is always great to see you.

00:00:03.367 --> 00:00:04.501
Is this your first time back?

00:00:04.501 --> 00:00:07.167
-This is my first time back
in the building.

00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:11.701
I've only gotten as far
as the wine bar close by.

00:00:11.701 --> 00:00:13.200
That's real.
-Wow.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:14.701
Are you are you emotional
right now?

00:00:14.701 --> 00:00:18.167
-You know, I'm not.
Being on this floor is okay.

00:00:18.167 --> 00:00:20.767
I'm sure I'd burst into tears --

00:00:20.767 --> 00:00:23.167
There's just a lot
of weird emotional baggage.

00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:24.200
-Yeah.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.634
-It was a lot of years
of my life.

00:00:25.634 --> 00:00:27.767
-I didn't know that you were
leaving "Saturday Night Live."

00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:28.968
I didn't know.

00:00:28.968 --> 00:00:31.167
That episode
came on, and I was like, "Wait.

00:00:31.167 --> 00:00:33.033
Is Cecily leaving?
Like, what's going on?"

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:36.234
-Yes. Although, we all knew.
It wasn't, like, a huge shock.

00:00:36.234 --> 00:00:39.868
But I think nobody --
I think there were still people

00:00:39.868 --> 00:00:41.400
that I love at the show
that were like,

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:43.000
"If we don't say it,
it won't happen."

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:44.434
-Yeah.
-"Then she'll stay."

00:00:44.434 --> 00:00:45.567
-Yeah, 'cause I thought maybe
just stay out for another --

00:00:45.567 --> 00:00:47.801
-It's worked for
nine and a half other years.

00:00:47.801 --> 00:00:49.133
-Lorne's good at
getting people to just go,

00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:50.501
"No, just stay
for another thing."

00:00:50.501 --> 00:00:51.567
But wow.
-Yeah.

00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:53.667
-It was --
That's a big move for you.

00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:55.567
-It was huge,
because, again, it was,

00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:58.901
You know, I was 28 once,
it turns out,

00:00:58.901 --> 00:01:00.067
when I started that show.

00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:01.634
I'm 78 now, so...

00:01:01.634 --> 00:01:03.300
-Oh, my God.
You look fantastic.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:05.000
-Oh, thank you.
A lot of surgeries.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.667
-I want your doctor's number,
yeah, really.

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:08.534
-A lot of doctors.
A couple have died.

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:11.000
-But you --
-Yeah.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.734
-But they were good people.
-They were great people.

00:01:12.734 --> 00:01:14.567
-They were great doctors.

00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:17.901
But you --
You're doing the show,

00:01:17.901 --> 00:01:19.868
and I go,
"Okay, I would lose it there.

00:01:19.868 --> 00:01:22.167
I would lose it in that episode.
I would lose it in that thing."

00:01:22.167 --> 00:01:24.300
And then Austin Butler
is the host,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.801
who was phenomenal as Elvis.

00:01:26.801 --> 00:01:27.868
-Unbelievable.

00:01:27.868 --> 00:01:30.968
-And he serenades you as Elvis.

00:01:30.968 --> 00:01:34.334
-I mean, that's the best
last gift on Earth.

00:01:34.334 --> 00:01:36.267
I think Lorne brought it up
in an elevator.

00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:38.067
I was, like, doing okay,

00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:39.968
and then Lorne, on Tuesday,
was like,

00:01:39.968 --> 00:01:41.334
"I think he's going to do

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:43.000
'Blue Christmas,'
if you'd like."

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.200
And I was just like, "Mm-hmm.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.400
Yeah, I think I would love
to --" Yeah.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:50.367
And I think that's the one thing
he asked to not do

00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:53.000
was to just sing as Elvis,
and we were like,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.267
"And can you sing as Elvis
for everybody?"

00:01:54.267 --> 00:01:56.000
-Yeah. And he did it.
-And he did it.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:57.334
-It was classic.
-Yes.

00:01:57.334 --> 00:02:00.868
I don't think I've cried
and hugged a host so much ever.

00:02:00.868 --> 00:02:02.968
[ Audience "awws" ]

00:02:02.968 --> 00:02:04.934
Thank you for that reaction.

00:02:04.934 --> 00:02:06.667
Yeah.
-You're very loving person.

00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:08.167
Did you -- You worked with
Matt Damon before.

00:02:08.167 --> 00:02:09.501
Did he host?
-Yes.

00:02:09.501 --> 00:02:13.400
He hosted a really great --
another Christmas show.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.367
And Bryan Tucker and I wrote a
Barbra Streisand-inspired

00:02:17.367 --> 00:02:19.167
cabaret sketch.

00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:22.133
It was strange to pitch to
Matt Damon.

00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:23.133
-Yeah. Well,
I think of Matt Damon,

00:02:23.133 --> 00:02:24.300
I think of singing and dancing.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.667
-"You're gonna be my
snarky, sexually fluid

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:29.434
ex-husband at the piano."

00:02:29.434 --> 00:02:30.634
But he was amazing.

00:02:30.634 --> 00:02:31.834
-He's great at everything
he does.

00:02:31.834 --> 00:02:33.000
-Yeah.

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.167
-Yeah, I get to see you
every now and then

00:02:35.167 --> 00:02:36.701
on these Verizon
commercials that'll pop up.

00:02:36.701 --> 00:02:39.667
-Sure. They play sometimes.
-Yeah, sometimes, they play.

00:02:39.667 --> 00:02:40.701
But it's good to see you.

00:02:40.701 --> 00:02:41.834
I want to see more
Cecily Strong.

00:02:41.834 --> 00:02:43.000
I love seeing you.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.100
Is this the first commercial
you ever did?

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.400
-No, no, no.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:48.834
I actually -- But my
first commercial never aired.

00:02:48.834 --> 00:02:50.734
-Oh. What was it for?

00:02:50.734 --> 00:02:54.000
-I mean, it wasn't an exciting
reason it never aired,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.434
but it was
a McDonald's commercial.

00:02:56.434 --> 00:03:00.400
I think I was 10 and I was -- I
just had to lick an ice cream --

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:02.000
I went in
for a different audition,

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.434
and the casting director
was like, "Hold on one second."

00:03:05.434 --> 00:03:07.200
-"I see something."
-Yeah.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.200
And then just had me --
I didn't have any lines.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:11.167
I think I just licked the cone.

00:03:11.167 --> 00:03:12.534
And then they said to me like,

00:03:12.534 --> 00:03:15.067
"Well, 39 cents must be
bad ice cream."

00:03:15.067 --> 00:03:16.300
I'm paraphrasing.
-Yeah.

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.033
-And then I went...

00:03:18.033 --> 00:03:19.968
And then they said,
"Let me rephrase that."

00:03:19.968 --> 00:03:21.234
And I went...

00:03:23.501 --> 00:03:24.667
-That's a good --
That's a big --

00:03:24.667 --> 00:03:26.234
-Well, I don't know
how good it was.

00:03:26.234 --> 00:03:28.734
It never aired.
-Never aired. No.

00:03:28.734 --> 00:03:31.534
I think McDonald's --
-They stopped serving ice cream.

00:03:31.534 --> 00:03:32.767
-Yeah.

00:03:32.767 --> 00:03:35.501
-That's why it's always
broken at McDonald's.

00:03:35.501 --> 00:03:36.901
Yeah, exactly right.

00:03:36.901 --> 00:03:39.267
Get Cecily in the spots
and fix that thing.

00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:41.067
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:41.067 --> 00:03:43.567
-Yeah.

00:03:43.567 --> 00:03:45.167
-I saw a nice article

00:03:45.167 --> 00:03:48.033
they did about you
in "Architectural Digest."

00:03:48.033 --> 00:03:50.667
-Oh, yeah.
-And very beautiful.

00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:53.968
-You read that often.
You're always --

00:03:53.968 --> 00:03:55.801
-Always reading that
and "Cigar Aficionado."

00:03:55.801 --> 00:03:56.868
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:56.868 --> 00:03:58.734
-They're the only
two magazines I read.

00:03:58.734 --> 00:04:01.033
Do they still make that one?
-You've gotten very classy.

00:04:01.033 --> 00:04:02.100
Yeah, of course they do.
-Thank you so much.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:03.701
-Yeah, of course they do.
But look at this.

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:06.701
This is your place right here.
-Yeah.

00:04:06.701 --> 00:04:09.801
-If you could see this,
these are all just wigs.

00:04:09.801 --> 00:04:11.934
-Yes.

00:04:11.934 --> 00:04:14.067
-See, now, I --
So, I had a question about this.

00:04:14.067 --> 00:04:17.467
This is -- Are these --
Well, what are these?

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:19.501
-"I have a question. Uh..."
-Yeah, that is my question.

00:04:19.501 --> 00:04:22.000
-So, I've always had
wigs at home,

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.834
but my -- I live
with my friend Kevin,

00:04:24.834 --> 00:04:30.100
who just started putting the
wigs on the things on the walls.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.667
I have a lot of animal busts,
it turns out.

00:04:31.667 --> 00:04:32.934
-Yeah.

00:04:32.934 --> 00:04:34.801
-And so we would put
the wigs on the animal bu--

00:04:34.801 --> 00:04:38.467
And then it just sort of became
the way of showcasing the wigs.

00:04:38.467 --> 00:04:39.767
-Now, do people
do people come over --

00:04:39.767 --> 00:04:42.300
-Put them on. Yes, of course.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:45.334
-Of course.
-What a silly question.

00:04:45.334 --> 00:04:46.434
-"What are you talking about?"

00:04:46.434 --> 00:04:47.701
So if I came over to the house,

00:04:47.701 --> 00:04:49.300
I'd just grab a wig
off the wall --

00:04:49.300 --> 00:04:50.701
-It just makes everything --

00:04:50.701 --> 00:04:54.100
Like, if you're playing games,
the games are more fun.

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:55.200
-Yeah, everyone --

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:56.801
-I like to choose
the wigs for --

00:04:56.801 --> 00:04:59.434
You know, like,
"You know who you are tonight?

00:04:59.434 --> 00:05:01.634
You are the
sassy business lady."

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:04.901
-Ooh, sassy business lady wig.
-Those are the short ones. Yeah.

00:05:04.901 --> 00:05:07.634
-But then I also
noticed this, as well.

00:05:07.634 --> 00:05:08.701
This is a bookcase.

00:05:08.701 --> 00:05:09.901
Of course, books,

00:05:09.901 --> 00:05:11.634
and then, down here, it's
just -- It's spare boots?

00:05:11.634 --> 00:05:12.767
-Oh, yes.

00:05:12.767 --> 00:05:16.200
So -- And that was
actually RuPaul suggested

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:19.868
if you've got wigs, you should
get heels in every size, too,

00:05:19.868 --> 00:05:25.167
because it also -- it just --
It changes up the atmosphere

00:05:25.167 --> 00:05:28.767
when you can put on a wig
and a thigh-high pair of boots.

00:05:28.767 --> 00:05:32.601
And everyone can wear them.
I have them in all sizes.

00:05:32.601 --> 00:05:36.133
-So, I cannot wait to be
invited over to the house.

00:05:36.133 --> 00:05:38.000
-The ones that are my size
are in my room.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.300
-No, you have your own.
Of course.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:40.701
It's not like bowling shoes.

00:05:40.701 --> 00:05:42.901
Whoever gets this,
this is their own shoe.

00:05:42.901 --> 00:05:45.467
-I have my own wig in my room,
I discovered, too.

00:05:45.467 --> 00:05:49.434
I was like, "Oh, I have this
little blond bob in my drawer."

00:05:49.434 --> 00:05:51.601
I was like,
"That is a funny choice I made."

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:54.167
-Yeah.
-I guess for an intrusion.

00:05:54.167 --> 00:05:55.701
I don't know. Yeah.

00:05:55.701 --> 00:05:58.634
"She's not here. I don't know."
Yeah.

00:05:58.634 --> 00:06:00.601
-You put on the wig and you're
like, "Where's Cecily?

00:06:00.601 --> 00:06:02.801
-"You're in the wrong house."

00:06:02.801 --> 00:06:04.467
-[ French accent ] She lives
in a different house.

00:06:04.467 --> 00:06:05.767
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-She does not live here.

00:06:05.767 --> 00:06:07.634
-[ French accent ]
I'm just a blond French woman.

00:06:07.634 --> 00:06:10.100
-I don't know. I don't --
-Yeah.

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:12.634
Put on a wig.
You can wear those heels.

00:06:12.634 --> 00:06:14.067
-Yeah, put on --
Wear some boots.

00:06:14.067 --> 00:06:15.601
Take them with you.
Yeah, please.

00:06:15.601 --> 00:06:16.901
-Yes.

00:06:16.901 --> 00:06:20.067
-I wanted to talk about,
also, you did a one-woman show

00:06:20.067 --> 00:06:23.300
that got great reviews,
and it's actually based on

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:24.801
"The Search for Signs of
Intelligent Life

00:06:24.801 --> 00:06:28.634
in the Universe," Lily Tomlin,
who originated it.

00:06:28.634 --> 00:06:30.734
-Written by Jane Wagner.
Yeah.

00:06:30.734 --> 00:06:33.534
-Legendary.
-Cuckoo that I would do that.

00:06:33.534 --> 00:06:35.667
-You crushed it, buddy.
You did a great job with that.

00:06:35.667 --> 00:06:37.734
-It was one of, like,

00:06:37.734 --> 00:06:39.801
one of the most
amazing experiences of my life.

00:06:39.801 --> 00:06:42.901
And Lily Tomlin was
at the opening in L.A.,

00:06:42.901 --> 00:06:44.968
but they didn't tell me
until that afternoon.

00:06:44.968 --> 00:06:46.167
-Look at this. I would have --

00:06:46.167 --> 00:06:50.100
-I mean, there are
a lot of tears in my eyes.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:51.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:51.300 --> 00:06:54.067
-Lily Tomlin is the greatest.
-The greatest.

00:06:54.067 --> 00:06:55.200
-She's the greatest.

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.133
-I don't think
I've ever been so nervous.

00:06:57.133 --> 00:06:58.734
And I got to do --
In curtain call,

00:06:58.734 --> 00:07:00.934
I got to --
And my director was like,

00:07:00.934 --> 00:07:03.300
"If you can't do it,
I can come help you out,

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:04.968
like, if you can't
get through it."

00:07:04.968 --> 00:07:07.234
And I was like,
"And the original Trudy..."

00:07:07.234 --> 00:07:08.334
-Yeah.

00:07:08.334 --> 00:07:11.467
-And I got to get
applause for Lily Tomlin.

00:07:11.467 --> 00:07:12.534
It was incredible.

00:07:12.534 --> 00:07:16.434
-I mean, did the play,
in some way,

00:07:16.434 --> 00:07:18.701
make you rethink everything
or think --

00:07:18.701 --> 00:07:20.701
-Why I left "SNL."
-No, serious --

00:07:20.701 --> 00:07:22.033
-No, no, no. It isn't really.

00:07:22.033 --> 00:07:25.000
-But it was good --
-I thought I got a good scoop.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:29.367
-I think, though, it's probably
I needed something else after,

00:07:29.367 --> 00:07:33.634
so I don't know that I would
jump into another one-woman show

00:07:33.634 --> 00:07:35.801
performed by an icon
for years anymore.

00:07:35.801 --> 00:07:37.534
I don't need it as much now

00:07:37.534 --> 00:07:40.200
that I'm not doing "SNL,"
if that makes sense.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:41.834
-But, I mean, you did that.

00:07:41.834 --> 00:07:43.234
I know you can sing because

00:07:43.234 --> 00:07:44.667
I've seen you on
"Saturday Night Live."

00:07:44.667 --> 00:07:46.367
I've done sketches with you
where you sang --

00:07:46.367 --> 00:07:48.100
-I think we have sung together.
-We've sung a couple duets.

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:49.267
-Yeah.
-Yes.

00:07:49.267 --> 00:07:50.868
Some of my favorite sketches.
-Here even. Yes.

00:07:50.868 --> 00:07:55.167
-Here even. Yes.
But you can really sing.

00:07:55.167 --> 00:07:58.701
-Well, I would say I felt like
I could really sing on "SNL."

00:07:58.701 --> 00:08:01.367
It's very different
when you're sitting next

00:08:01.367 --> 00:08:02.501
to Kristin Chenoweth or --

00:08:02.501 --> 00:08:03.667
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:03.667 --> 00:08:05.100
That's why we don't hang out,
'cause I go,

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:06.467
"Yeah, no, I don't want --"
Yeah.

00:08:06.467 --> 00:08:09.033
-Yeah, that's why you don't.
She was asking about that.

00:08:09.033 --> 00:08:10.434
-Yeah.
-Okay. I'll tell her.

00:08:10.434 --> 00:08:11.567
-Yeah.

00:08:11.567 --> 00:08:12.701
-But, I mean,
there was one number,

00:08:12.701 --> 00:08:16.167
I was, like --
Literally, I'm chained to

00:08:16.167 --> 00:08:19.567
Ariana DeBose and Dove Cameron,
trying to dance, like --

00:08:19.567 --> 00:08:23.133
And if I take one bad step,
I'm going to bring down

00:08:23.133 --> 00:08:24.601
Ariana DeBose and Dove Cameron.

00:08:24.601 --> 00:08:25.701
-No. They're royalty.

00:08:25.701 --> 00:08:27.200
You can't take down
Ariana DeBose.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:28.400
No, not at all.
-Right.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:29.934
-But you hold your own.
More than hold your own.

00:08:29.934 --> 00:08:32.033
You're fantastic.
-Well, I stay standing.

00:08:32.033 --> 00:08:34.300
-No. You are phenomenal.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:37.033
Can we say what's going on
in Season 2 of "Schmigadoon!"?

00:08:37.033 --> 00:08:39.033
-Yes. So, Season 2,

00:08:39.033 --> 00:08:41.701
Josh and Melissa
have gotten out of Schmigadoon.

00:08:41.701 --> 00:08:42.901
They're married. They're happy.

00:08:42.901 --> 00:08:47.167
The doldrums of regular life
starts to set in,

00:08:47.167 --> 00:08:49.901
so they decide they're going
to head back to Schmigadoon.

00:08:49.901 --> 00:08:52.400
But they get there
and they learn

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.968
you can never go back
to Schmigadoon.

00:08:54.968 --> 00:08:57.467
And they find themselves
in Schmicago.

00:08:57.467 --> 00:09:00.467
-Schmicago.
-Of course.

00:09:00.467 --> 00:09:02.701
Who is the creator of the show?
Who is --

00:09:02.701 --> 00:09:03.934
-Cinco.
-Man, oh, man.

00:09:03.934 --> 00:09:06.167
-Cinco Paul. Yeah.
-Man, talented.

00:09:06.167 --> 00:09:08.634
Oh, he's a genius.
-Genius. Genius.

00:09:08.634 --> 00:09:11.234
-But these musicals and this
life is a little bit darker.

00:09:11.234 --> 00:09:13.200
-Oh, it's darker.
-Grittier.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.934
-Everybody
has much less clothing on.

00:09:15.934 --> 00:09:17.300
Not us.
-Yeah.

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:20.334
But it's like
those plays and like

00:09:20.334 --> 00:09:23.167
"Hair," "Sweeney Todd" --

00:09:23.167 --> 00:09:26.767
-"Cabaret," "Sweeney Todd."
-Just darker, darker stuff.

00:09:26.767 --> 00:09:28.667
-Yes.
-But it's so funny.

00:09:28.667 --> 00:09:30.701
And Keegan-Michael Key
is unbelievable, as well.

00:09:30.701 --> 00:09:32.467
You guys are great together.
-I love him so much.

00:09:32.467 --> 00:09:34.868
This season, I got
to spend more time with him,

00:09:34.868 --> 00:09:38.367
and, I mean, we just made
each other laugh every day.

00:09:38.367 --> 00:09:40.467
If they didn't yell "cut,"

00:09:40.467 --> 00:09:41.767
we were just going to keep
going, you know.

00:09:41.767 --> 00:09:43.067
-Two of the
funniest people ever.

00:09:43.067 --> 00:09:44.868
I also heard -- Is Marty Short
in this season, as well?

00:09:44.868 --> 00:09:46.734
Yes. I didn't get to,
like, work on set.

00:09:46.734 --> 00:09:48.767
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Oh, please. It's not --

00:09:48.767 --> 00:09:50.467
-Oh, you know of him.
-Oh, you've heard of him?

00:09:50.467 --> 00:09:52.968
-You should check him out.
He's very good.

00:09:52.968 --> 00:09:54.367
-I don't want to
give that much applause.

00:09:54.367 --> 00:09:56.267
He's not here. Yeah.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:56.267 --> 00:09:57.934
-Well, I want to show a clip.

00:09:57.934 --> 00:10:00.601
Here's Cecily Strong in
the new season of "Schmigadoon!"

00:10:00.601 --> 00:10:01.734
Take a look at this.

00:10:01.734 --> 00:10:03.000
♪♪

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:06.000
-I feel like this is around
where we saw the bridge?

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:07.667
-Oh! Yes.

00:10:07.667 --> 00:10:09.734
I think I remember this tree.

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:13.667
-You remember this tree
from two years ago?

00:10:13.667 --> 00:10:16.567
-I just really want to find it.

00:10:16.567 --> 00:10:19.100
-Don't worry. We will find it.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:21.601
Oh, I got a
really good feeling...

00:10:23.133 --> 00:10:25.000
about this direction.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:27.667
-Totally. Yeah, me too. Yep.

00:10:30.534 --> 00:10:32.567
-Maybe we're not supposed
to find it again.

00:10:32.567 --> 00:10:34.334
Maybe it's one of those places
that you only go once

00:10:34.334 --> 00:10:35.634
and never return.

00:10:35.634 --> 00:10:37.067
-Oh, like SoulCycle.

00:10:37.067 --> 00:10:41.367
-Yeah, exactly.
Like SoulCycle. Cecily Strong.

00:10:41.367 --> 00:10:45.567
Season 2 of "Schmigadoon!"
is now streaming on Apple TV+,

00:10:45.567 --> 00:10:47.334
and she is phenomenal in it.

00:10:47.334 --> 00:10:48.934
New episodes air
every Wednesday.

